---
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import Stitch from "../components/ui/Stitch.astro";

// Deliberately a curated table, not a database. Per the site's evidence
// policy (FACTS.md: citation adjacent to claim, 6-month recheck, 48-hour
// correction), every row here is a real maintenance commitment — cap this
// list at roughly 10 rows. If it wants to grow past that, the right fix is
// rethinking the architecture (e.g. per-state pages with named owners), not
// silently growing this table past what one person can keep current.
---

<Page
	title="The state landscape — Automate Ethically"
	description="Every state is writing these rules right now. Here's what's already real, and where to watch what's still moving."
	ogImage="/og/states.png"
>
	<section class="ae-hero" aria-labelledby="states-title">
		<p class="ae-eyebrow">The landscape</p>
		<!-- DRAFT: rewrite — copy task 25: /states framing -->
		<h1 id="states-title">Every state is writing these rules.</h1>
		<p class="ae-hero-sub">
			1,561 AI-related bills were introduced in 45 states this session
			(<a href="https://www.multistate.ai/artificial-intelligence-ai-legislation">multistate.ai</a>,
			Mar 2026) — far more than one site can track reliably. The
			<a href="https://www.multistate.ai/artificial-intelligence-ai-legislation">multistate.ai
			tracker</a> is the live source of truth for everything moving right
			now. Below is a short, hand-checked list of what's already real.
		</p>
	</section>

	<Stitch />

	<section class="ae-section" aria-label="State-by-state examples">
		<div class="ae-container">
			<div class="ae-prose">
				<table>
					<thead>
						<tr>
							<th>State</th>
							<th>Status</th>
							<th>Checked</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr>
							<td><strong>Montana</strong></td>
							<td>
								HB 178 already requires human review of AI-driven decisions
								made by state agencies — the closest existing precedent to
								what this movement is asking cities and counties to adopt.
								(<a href="https://www.ebglaw.com/insights/publications/ai-legislation-2026-legislative-wrap-up">Epstein
								Becker Green</a>)
							</td>
							<td>Aug 2026</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><strong>Colorado</strong></td>
							<td>
								An amended AI Act, effective January 1, 2027, preserves a
								right to "meaningful human review of adverse automated
								decisions" for consequential decisions — employment, housing,
								lending, healthcare, and similar. Not yet in effect.
								(<a href="https://www.hunton.com/privacy-and-cybersecurity-law-blog/colorado-ai-act-amended-and-effective-date-delayed">Hunton
								Andrews Kurth</a>)
							</td>
							<td>Aug 2026</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
				<p class="ae-aside">
					This table is short on purpose — every row here is a promise we
					keep checking. See our
					<a href="/facts">correction policy</a> for how that works, or the
					<a href="https://www.multistate.ai/artificial-intelligence-ai-legislation">multistate.ai
					tracker</a> for the fuller, faster-moving picture.
				</p>
			</div>

			<p class="ae-plain ae-states-cta">
				Don't see your state? That's the point — it isn't decided yet.
			</p>
			<div class="ae-cta-row">
				<a class="ae-btn ae-btn--primary" href="/act#lead">Tell us what's happening in your town</a>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</Page>
